<h2>The allegations and the investigation</h2>
<p>The prosecutrix alleged that on 25 October 2025 she was invited by the co-accused, with whom she was in a relationship, to a party at a Karol Bagh hotel, where she became intoxicated. The petitioner is alleged to have displayed a pistol to a cab driver and then threatened her with it in the hotel room; a drug was allegedly administered; and the co-accused is alleged to have had sexual relations with her without consent. The medico-legal examination recorded recent sexual assault. The co-accused surrendered, was arrested on 20 November 2025, and stands charge-sheeted. The petitioner, the prosecution said, absconded and switched off his phone before joining the investigation on 31 January 2026 under the court&rsquo;s directions. Hotel CCTV showing the three entering and leaving is on record; the pistol is not recovered.</p>

<h2>How the Court weighed it</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Gravity first.</strong> Offences under Sections 64(1), 75, 123 and 351(2) BNS, with a firearm allegedly brandished, placed the case in the category where Srikant Upadhyay counsels restraint.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation incomplete.</strong> The petitioner&rsquo;s precise role was still being investigated and a supplementary chargesheet was proposed; the unrecovered pistol kept custodial interrogation on the table.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Antecedents noted, not overstated.</strong> Two FIRs of 2020 were treated as relevant background, with the express caveat that registration alone <em>&ldquo;does not establish his guilt.&rdquo;</em></div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Result.</strong> <em>&ldquo;I do not consider this to be a fit case for the grant of anticipatory bail.&rdquo;</em> Application dismissed; observations confined to the application.</div>
</div>

<h2>The petitioner&rsquo;s case, and why it failed</h2>
<p>The defence pressed youth, deep roots in society, an aged mother, and the fact that the prosecutrix attributed no forcible act of rape to the petitioner himself. It also argued deliberate implication and pointed to similar complaints by the prosecutrix against others. None of this was dismissed as irrelevant; it was simply outweighed. The order reflects a consistent pattern in Delhi anticipatory bail practice: where the allegation involves a weapon that has not been recovered, pre-arrest protection would place the most important recovery beyond the investigation&rsquo;s reach. The Court also followed the line in <strong>Sumitha Pradeep v. Arun Kumar C.K.</strong> — quoted in Krishna Mohan Reddy — that cooperation with the investigation does not neutralise the need for custodial interrogation where the probe is at a crucial stage.</p>

<h2>Reading the order in practice</h2>
<p>For advocates assessing a Section 482 BNSS petition, this order is a useful checklist of what must be addressed head-on: the status of recoveries, the stage of the chargesheet against the applicant specifically, antecedents however stale, and conduct between the FIR and the first appearance — here, the five weeks of alleged absconding weighed visibly. Where those factors are adverse, the stronger course may be to prepare for regular bail with the benefit of the chargesheet, rather than to accumulate reasoned refusals at the pre-arrest stage.</p>
<div class="note">An anticipatory bail refusal is not a finding of guilt. The Court expressly limited its observations to the disposal of the application. Case details are as recorded in the order dated 4 November 2026.</div>
